## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design crash‑consistent data paths in persistent memory, a common production issue when power failures occur during in‑flight operations.

## Key Concepts

- Durability guarantees for PMEM writes
- Memory ordering: release/acquire, fences, cacheline flushes
- Recovery via redo log and slot sequence counters
- ABA avoidance in a concurrent ring
- Testing with fault injection and crash replay

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-code: enqueue/dequeue with PMEM flush/fence
// Slot: { data, seq, valid }
void enqueue(T v){
  size_t t = tail.fetch_add(1);
  Slot *s = &ring[t % CAP];
  s->data = v; // write data
  clwb(&s->data, sizeof(T));
  fence(); // ensure data persists
  s->seq = t; // publish with durable tail update (release)
  clwb(&s->seq, sizeof(size_t));
  fence();
  // publish tail already happens via atomic tail update
}

bool dequeue(T *out){
  size_t h0 = head.load();
  Slot *s = &ring[h0 % CAP];
  if (s->seq != h0) return false;
  *out = s->data;
  s->valid = false;
  clwb(&s->valid, sizeof(bool));
  fence();
  head.fetch_add(1);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle power loss during the publish vs data write steps?
- How would you validate recovery correctness across multiple restart scenarios?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of crash-consistency semantics, cross-socket memory ordering on x86-64 systems, and practical persistent memory programming patterns.

## Key Concepts
- **Persistent Memory Semantics**: Understanding cache line flushes (clwb/clflushopt) and memory ordering fences (sfence/mfence)
- **Crash-Consistency Guarantees**: Ensuring the queue survives power loss without data corruption
- **Sequence Counter Design**: Handling wrap-around and enforcing total ordering across concurrent producers
- **Recovery Mechanisms**: Log-based recovery and ring buffer reconstruction after failure
- **Correctness Under Contention**: Two producers racing to reserve slots requires careful synchronization

## Code Example
```c
// Enqueue: reserve tail slot atomically
uint64_t tail = atomic_fetch_add(&q->tail, 1);
Slot* slot = &q->slots[tail % N];

// Spin until slot is available
while (atomic_load(&slot->seq) != tail) { cpu_pause(); }

// Write payload to PMEM
memcpy(slot->payload, data, len);

// Persist in order: payload then sequence
atomic_store(&slot->seq, tail + 1);
clflushopt(slot);      // flush cache line to PMEM
sfence();               // ensure durability
```

